course: dictionary
lesson: 3253
title: Plants that live on plants
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An epiphyte is a plant that grows on another plant, usually a tree, for support. It is not a parasite, because it does not take food from the plant it grows on.
explain: Epiphytes get water and nutrients from the air, from rain, and from bits of rubbish that collect around them. «Epiphytes such as orchids, ferns and bromeliads live on branches high up, catching rain and fallen bits instead of rooting in the soil.»
explain: Living high up helps them reach sunlight, which is why many epiphytes are found in the rainforest canopy, the high layer of leaves at the top of the trees.
fact: An epiphyte is a plant that grows on another plant, usually a tree, for support.
fact: Epiphytes do not take food from their host; a parasite does take food from its host.
fact: Many orchids, bromeliads, mosses and ferns are epiphytes.
fact: Epiphytes get water and nutrients from the air, rain and bits of rubbish, not from the soil.
fact: The phrases to know are «epiphytic plants» and «epiphytes in the rainforest canopy».
exercise: What is an epiphyte? | A fish that lives in rivers | *A plant that grows on another plant for support but does not take its food | A tree that grows in deserts | A plant that grows on the ground and steals food from other plants
exercise: Where do the epiphytes in «catching rain and fallen bits instead of rooting in the soil» get their water? | From a river nearby | From the sea | *From rain and fallen bits | From the soil through their roots
exercise: Why is an epiphyte not a parasite? | It can only grow in deserts | *It does not take food from the tree it grows on | It takes food from the tree it lives on | It lives underground
test: Why do many epiphytes grow high on tree branches? | To keep dry in the sea | To hide from animals in the soil | To eat the tree's leaves | *To reach sunlight
test: Which of these is an epiphyte? | A carrot in a field | A cactus in the desert | An oak root in the soil | *An orchid growing on a branch
exam: Where do epiphytes get their water and nutrients, and how is this different from a parasite? | From the sea, like a fish | From the soil only, like a carrot | From the host's roots, which harms the host | *From air, rain and bits of rubbish, without harming the host
